  "tagline": "Write SQL. Get typed Zig. Check it before it runs.",
  "lead": "sqlz is a SQL toolkit for Zig, inspired by Rust's SQLx. It checks your queries against a schema rebuilt from your migrations, generates typed bindings, and runs them on SQLite or PostgreSQL. No ORM, no query builder, no database needed at build time.",
  "install": "zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/westack-it/sqlz",
  "status": "Early development · Zig 0.17",
},
---

[]($section.id('proof'))
- Open source, MIT
- SQLite 3.45–3.53
- PostgreSQL 15–18
- Offline checking, no live database
- Backends lazy and off by default

# [Checked at build time]($section.id('feature-checked').data('icon','shield-check'))
`zig build` parses every query with pinned `libpg_query` and checks it against
your schema. Unknown columns, wrong parameter types and nullability mistakes
fail the build with source spans.

# [Schema from migrations]($section.id('feature-offline').data('icon','git-branch'))
The checker replays your migration history into an in-memory catalog. CI
checks the same schema as your laptop, without credentials or a running
database.

# [Typed bindings]($section.id('feature-typed').data('icon','zap'))
Named parameters become a params struct, result columns become a row struct.
Rows borrow from the driver; converting to owned rows is explicit.

# [Two ways to write queries]($section.id('feature-authoring').data('icon','book-open'))
Keep SQL in named `.sql` files, or declare a `sqlz.Query` next to the code
that runs it. Both are checked the same way.

# [Pay only for what you use]($section.id('feature-lazy').data('icon','package'))
SQLite (via zqlite) and PostgreSQL (via pg.zig) are lazy dependencies. A
disabled backend is never fetched, compiled or linked.

# [Bring your own `std.Io`]($section.id('feature-io').data('icon','server'))
Runtime handles take the application's `std.Io`, so sqlz runs on
`std.Io.Threaded` or a third-party runtime such as zio.

# [What is sqlz?]($section.id('about'))
sqlz keeps your application close to handwritten SQL. You write a query, the
build checks it and hands you a typed Zig declaration:

```sql
-- sqlz.name: find_user
-- sqlz.backends: sqlite, postgres
-- sqlz.cardinality: optional

SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE id = :id;
```

```zig
var found = (try sqlz.unwrap(find_user.fetchOptional(&conn, .{ .id = 1 }))) orelse
    return error.UserMissing;
defer found.deinit();
const user = found.row(); // struct { id: i64, name: []const u8 }
```

>[]($block.attrs('warning'))
>sqlz is pre-release. Checked queries and both runtimes work for the current
>SQL subset; running migrations against a database is not implemented yet,
>and the public API will change.

Follow the [quickstart]($link.page('quickstart')), or read the
[documentation]($link.page('docs')) to see how it works.
